    "# Lab Report — Zero-Shot, One-Shot and Few-Shot Prompting\n",
    "\n",
    "## Objective\n",
    "\n",
    "The objective of this lab was to understand how different prompting techniques influence the responses generated by Large Language Models (LLMs). I tested three approaches — Zero-Shot, One-Shot and Few-Shot — using football-related questions and the OpenAI API.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Experiments and Observations\n",
    "\n",
    "**Version 1 — Zero-Shot Prompting**\n",
    "\n",
    "I asked the model who won the Portuguese Primeira Liga in 2010, without providing any examples.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Result:** \"The Portuguese Primeira Liga 2010 was won by SL Benfica.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "The model answered correctly using its existing knowledge. However, the response followed a natural sentence structure rather than a predefined format.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Version 2 — One-Shot Prompting**\n",
    "\n",
    "I provided one example: the winner of the 1994 FIFA World Cup (Brazil), formatted as \"Team: Brazil.\" I then asked about the 1998 FIFA World Cup.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Result:** \"Team: France.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "The model answered correctly and followed the exact response structure demonstrated in the example.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Version 3 — Few-Shot Prompting**\n",
    "\n",
    "I provided three examples of FIFA World Cup winners: Spain (2010), Italy (2006) and Argentina (1986). I then asked about the 1990 FIFA World Cup.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Result:** \"Team: West Germany.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Again, the model provided the correct answer and maintained the expected format.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Key Findings\n",
    "\n",
    "All three prompting techniques produced factually correct responses. The main difference observed was how the model structured its answers.\n",
    "\n",
    "Zero-Shot demonstrated that an LLM can answer factual questions without additional examples. One-Shot showed that a single example can guide the output format, while Few-Shot demonstrated that multiple examples can reinforce a consistent response pattern.\n",
    "\n",
    "No hallucinations or factual errors were observed in these three tests. However, because the questions were different and relatively straightforward, the experiments do not establish that one technique is more accurate than another.\n",
    "\n",
    "## What I Learned\n",
    "\n",
    "This lab helped me understand that **Prompt Engineering is not only about asking questions, but also about guiding how an AI model interprets instructions and presents information.**\n",
    "\n",
    "I learned that examples can be a powerful way to establish a desired response structure without changing the underlying Python code or model.\n",
    "\n",
    "I also understood the importance of designing controlled experiments. To compare the accuracy of different prompting strategies reliably, I should use the same questions, consistent evaluation criteria and multiple test cases.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "My main takeaway is that **Zero-Shot can be sufficient for simple tasks, while One-Shot and Few-Shot can be useful when consistent formatting or specific response patterns are required.**\n",
    "\n",
    "These techniques are particularly relevant to real-world AI applications, such as classification, structured data extraction and football scouting reports, where predictable and standardized outputs can improve the usefulness of an AI system."
   ]
  },
